A||||11||camon||What a job!||||10||
Z||000000||camon||12-08-2001||03:18 AM||||Fuel pump is now on the car!!!<P>I lost one of the nuts that hold the tank strap on. It's the nut that goes on the "T" bolt! What size is that nut so I can get one today sometime and tighten the front tank strap?????<P>The good news is.....<BR>The Fuel pump was my problem. After getting erything back togather it fired right up. Good thing it did because this job had me so ticked off (many times) that if it still wouldn't have started I would have threw my tool box through the windshield!<P>The bad news is......<BR>Man what a crappy job. What made it even worse is there was a 1/2 tank of fuel in it (the pump couldn't go with the cfuelo gauge almost on "E"). To make things worse I did it in front of my house by pushing the passenger side onto the side walk, didn't feel safe doing it on jacks...because of the traffic (I did us the jack and stands to get the tank out from under the car though. It is even worse believe it or not. It got dark about 1/4 into the job (while dropping the tank)Worked by drop light the rest of the way (a good 3 hours). Had no choice they are calling for rain this weekend in my area...so it had to be done today!<P>Well this was the roughest job I ever attempted on a car (don't know if it was the night or the traffic of the fact everything was about 2" from my face.....but rough ohh god was it rough!!!). <P>Thanks to everyone that gave me advice and also the people that posted responses that I came across using the search feature (I would have been completely lost without the info I obtained through the search feature).<P>Well how bout that nut size (for the "T" bolt)???????<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||10
Z||000001||85 gt fiero||12-08-2001||09:08 AM||||i replaced a fuel pump on my old 84 when i was 15, man what a pain took me 7 hours to do that and i found it hard to get the hosses back on the metal lines coming out of the gas tank. But it purred afterthat until the tranny went out <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.    About the nut i dont remember what size but it was between 11-15 mm i think i may be thinking of the wrong bolt though.  Randy<P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES AND SOON TO BE BRIGHT YELLOW||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jake_Dragon||12-08-2001||09:59 AM||||I did my 85 with a full tank.<BR>Your right it was a pain, February on cold concrete.<BR>Man it must have been about 65 out,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I Love Florida.<BR>I did jack that sucker up as far as the jack stands would go.<BR>Took about 3 hours.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||12-08-2001||12:04 PM||||I had to drop my tank to fix a few leaks. It was 1/2 full at the time also <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">. Everything ended up covered in gas. I was in a garage, gas was EVERYWHERE and you could smell it from the end of my street. Lucky I didn't blow myself up by droping a tool and making a spark. The fumes really got to me during the operation  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">.<P>Droping a fiero tank is something I hope to never do again.||||reg||10
Z||000004||JohnF||12-08-2001||12:16 PM||||Try 13MM.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001112&p=56509969&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||10
Z||000005||camon||12-08-2001||01:08 PM||||Sorry I wasn't clearer about the strap nut.<BR>I need to know the tread diameter. I tried another 13mm nut that I found in my parts box but the threads must not be the same because it started but couldn't get it to tighen (after about one turn it was too tight to turn). So I quess I nee the "T" bolt diameter????<P>Yeah those hoses were a pain to to get off and an even bigger pain to get on (mostly because of the clamp locations). That very top hose (the one that goes through the fire wall I got good and tight just couldn't get it on very far (gotta keep my eye on it for leaks (BTW...where does that hose go, and is it under heavy pressure?). The only hose I didn't remove was the double clamp hose (fuel filter). Heck the clamps were against metal so I couldn't get them. I took the fuel filter off at the fuel filter nut.<P>I didn't have any major gas spills (thank god). But then I was very slow and cautious while dropping and movong the tank.<P>If I ever need to do this again...I may think twice about it (it may goto a repair shop next time, unless it happenes at a better location (like in a parking lot...or garage).<P>Well later on!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||12-08-2001||07:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by camon:<B>I didn't have any major gas spills (thank God). But then I was very slow and cautious...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>If there was ever a time for "slow and cautious,"  this is one of them.  Car on stands, man under car, wrestling heavy awkward stuff that sloshes,  fuel around... yeah, you can envision all kinds of bad scenarios.  Thanks for taking your time and playing it safe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!  <P>Wish I could help you with the nut size, but I have never had to do the job you just described.  All the jobs I have done so far have been tankless ones  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I hope someone who knows will chime in soon with the thread size.  I like to take notes whenever I do a job the first time, recording socket sizes and so forth (occasionally weighing some of the bigger pieces).  Makes it just that much easier the next time!  <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000007||Phil||12-08-2001||07:15 PM||||I dropped the fuel tank once ( to fix a leak) , got it all back in and then discovered I had forgotten to plug the wiring harness back onto the pump. I learned some new cuss words that day.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carwreathsig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||camon||12-10-2001||02:03 AM||||Went into home depot on Sunday afternoon and played grab-man (grabed like 6 different metric nut sizes from 7mm-13mm).<P>The correct thread size (or metric nut to buy for these "T" bolts) is the 8mm nut!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||10
Z||000009||ITB 2M4||12-10-2001||02:18 AM||||As someone who has pulled the gas tank on an '84 many times, here's a little hint to help get those hoses back on afterwards.<P>A little bit of petroleum jelly, on the inside of the hoses and the outside of tank where they connect, and the slip right on much easier (Easier being a relative term when talking Fieros!) than without the stuff.  And being petroleum based, they won't hurt the fuel system or emissions.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||10
Z||000010||LZeitgeist||12-10-2001||09:04 AM||||Had to drop the fuel tank in Dad's '88 Coupe - jacked it up and set it on 4 ramps and got under it that way.<P>PITA to get it up there, but much safer than other ways we thought of, and lots of room to work under there, too.<P>Wasn't as bad a job as I had always heard it to be. <P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show 2001||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rumor||12-10-2001||04:12 PM||||I will replacing my pump in 1-2 weeks time.   I will measure the nut for you.<BR>||||reg||1
